import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How MoneyMattersDaily researches, writes, and reviews its content."
>
  <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>

  <p>
    This page explains how our content gets made, what we check before
    publishing, and where our current process still has gaps.
  </p>

  <h2>How articles are produced</h2>
  <p>
    Articles are drafted, then reviewed for accuracy before publishing —
    checking that figures, rate ranges, and rules referenced are correct as
    of the stated "last updated" date, and that any claim tied to a law,
    tax rule, or rate is either sourced to an official or primary source or
    clearly framed as a general range rather than a fixed number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some of our drafting and editing work is done with AI assistance. We
    don't treat that as a shortcut around quality — see
    <a href="/about/">About</a> for what we're actually optimizing for.
    Whether a piece involved AI assistance isn't the thing that determines
    whether it's useful; whether it's accurate, specific to your situation,
    and worth your time is.
  </p>

  <h2>Sourcing</h2>
  <p>
    Where a claim depends on a law, tax rule, interest rate, fee, or
    product term, we aim to link to or cite the primary source — a
    government agency, regulator, central bank, tax authority, fund
    prospectus, or the provider's own terms page — rather than another
    blog's summary of it.
  </p>

  <h2>"Reviewed by" and "Last updated"</h2>
  <p>
    We only add a "Reviewed by" credit to an article when a specific,
    qualified reviewer has actually reviewed that piece — we don't apply
    it as a blanket label. Most articles today don't carry one, and that's
    an accurate reflection of our current process, not an oversight.
  </p>
  <p>
    Articles that reference figures likely to change — rates, contribution
    limits, thresholds — carry a "last updated" note. When we revise an
    article in a way that changes its substance (not a typo fix), we
    update that date and note what changed.
  </p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>
    We don't accept payment for favorable coverage or specific rankings.
    We don't write "best X" content for the purpose of driving affiliate
    clicks — see <a href="/about/">About</a> for more on that. If that
    ever changes for a specific piece of content, we'll disclose it
    plainly on that page, not bury it in a footer.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    We get things wrong sometimes. See our
    <a href="/corrections-policy/">corrections policy</a> for how we
    handle that, or use our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> to flag
    an error directly.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
